From 09013716e833a42f2eb1562c7cdf4266560cea2d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pavel Artsishevsky <polter.rnd@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 19:00:53 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat: add GNOME auth dialog helper for VPN secrets

NetworkManager asks gnome-shell's secret agent for VPN secrets whenever
an editor opens a connection, and the agent runs the binary named by the
[GNOME] auth-dialog key of the service file. Add nm-amneziawg-auth-dialog
(based on tigro's Fedora patch) so GNOME Settings can read the stored
private and preshared keys instead of showing empty fields.

The helper needs to cope with how gnome-shell drives it:

- gnome-shell can close the read end of the helper's stderr pipe. A
  diagnostic written then raises SIGPIPE and kills the helper, which the
  shell reports as INTERNAL_ERROR, so GetSecrets fails and the editors
  show an empty private key. Ignore SIGPIPE and keep the startup message
  at g_debug.
- Reply with the secrets the agent handed over. Tested: with an empty
  reply the editors open with empty private and preshared key fields.
- Stay alive after the reply until the agent sends QUIT. The agent treats
  the helper exiting as the end of the request. Builds that wait for QUIT
  show the keys in GNOME Settings; builds without the wait have not.
- Only exit non-zero when interaction or new secrets were requested
  (-i/-r). A missing key on a plain lookup must not fail GetSecrets, or
  the editor loses the other stored secrets too.

Also log the private-key state in NeedSecrets (tigro's debug aid).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
